    "audioUrl": "https://ai-english-master-1473543848.cos.ap-beijing.myqcloud.com/audio/cet-6/cet6-202406-1.mp3",
    "transcript": "<p class=\"text-xs text-outline mb-2\">※ 原文由真题解析整理，供对照参考。</p>\n<h4 class=\"font-title-md text-on-surface mt-4 mb-2\">Long Conversation One</h4>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> Thank you for meeting with me, Stephen, at such a short notice.</p>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> Not a problem, Margaret. Now please give me some good news. Have you agreed to my last proposal?</p>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> I have indeed and I wish to sign the agreement, pending one small change to be made a contract.</p>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> Margaret, we've been through this for almost a year now, back and forth making alterations. Are you sure you want to make a sponsorship deal for your clients or not? I ask this because frankly, some people at my end are running out of patience.</p>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> I understand your concerns, but as I'm sure you understand, we hold our clients' best interests to be of the utmost concern. We therefore comb through the fine details of all contracts. Rest assured we all appreciate your firm's patience.</p>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> Okay, fine. So what changes do you wish to make?</p>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> Essentially, we would like the new deal to exclude the Middle East. That's all.</p>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> The Middle East? Why?</p>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> My client has a couple of other prospective marketing deals from companies in the Middle East. Those offers, should they materialize, would exclusively employ my client's image in the Middle East only. Therefore, in order to avoid any conflict, we would need to ensure that both marketing campaigns do not overlap geographically.</p>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> What business sector in the Middle East are we talking about here?</p>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> Real estate.</p>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> Well, that should be okay then. So long as the product is very different from our food and beverage market, there should be no conflict of interest. Nevertheless, I will have to run this through my people. I don't foresee any problem, though. The Middle East is a negligible market for us. But I still need to check this with a couple of departments.</p>\n<h4 class=\"font-title-md text-on-surface mt-4 mb-2\">Long Conversation Two</h4>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> Our colleague Tom has been talking non-stop about a rare astronomic phenomenon that's going to occur soon. He says it's called a comet, or comet-like object, that will come closest to Earth in more than 100 years.</p>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> It will be the most formidable of its kind in over a century.</p>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> I've heard about this too. We're all thrilled by this rare astronomic phenomenon. We're enthusiastic about big science-related stories.</p>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> We're even joined by astronomers all across North America.</p>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> But what will it actually look like to us regular folks observing from Earth?</p>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> Most people will see just a blur. But those who climb to nearby heights or use professional equipment will see more. Perhaps even stars or the edge of our galaxy.</p>\n<p><strong>W:</strong> So what should regular folks do to get a good view?</p>\n<p><strong>M:</strong> Tom also mentioned that phone apps can help people locate the object in the sky. He suggested that regular people should make use of phone apps.</p>\n<h4 class=\"font-title-md text-on-surface mt-4 mb-2\">Passage One</h4>\n<p>Should consumers be warned against ultra-processed foods? The question is dividing nutrition experts. Some researchers believe that there is not yet sufficient scientific consensus on the links between ultra-processed foods and poor health outcomes to justify warnings to consumers. Others argue that the evidence is already strong enough to act. Ultra-processed foods are defined not by the degree of industrial processing, but by the extent of chemical alteration. This means that foods which undergo complex chemical processes are classified as ultra-processed regardless of how they are made. Critics of the classification argue that the focus on processing might distract from more important nutritional factors like sugar, salt and fat content. They worry this could mislead consumers who need clear guidance.</p>\n<h4 class=\"font-title-md text-on-surface mt-4 mb-2\">Passage Two</h4>\n<p>Have you ever wondered why some of the world's most creative minds seem to flourish within strict constraints? It turns out that constraints can actually boost creativity rather than limit it. Research shows that when people face limitations—such as time pressure or resource scarcity—they are forced to think more innovatively. Studies of poets, artists and scientists have found that those who embrace constraints tend to produce more original work. The reason is that constraints narrow the scope of possibilities, allowing the mind to focus more deeply on promising ideas. Creative achievements often come not from unlimited freedom, but from working productively within given boundaries. History offers countless examples of masterpieces created under tight deadlines or strict formal rules. People often thrive best when constrained.</p>\n<h4 class=\"font-title-md text-on-surface mt-4 mb-2\">Recording One</h4>\n<p>In today's lecture, we will explore how effective managers handle workplace conflict. Conflict is a natural part of any organization where people with different backgrounds and perspectives work together. The first step in managing conflict is to understand its root causes, which often lie in misunderstandings or competing interests. Managers who ignore early signs of tension often see small disagreements escalate into serious disputes. Research indicates that the degree of difference and similarity between the two sides determines the appropriate conflict resolution strategy. When parties share common ground, collaboration is usually the most effective approach. When interests fundamentally clash, however, managers must help both sides accept a compromise they can live with. The lecture concludes that skilled conflict management turns disagreement into a source of innovation and growth.</p>\n<h4 class=\"font-title-md text-on-surface mt-4 mb-2\">Recording Two</h4>\n<p>The Darwin Tree of Life Project aims to sequence the genomes of all living species in Britain and Ireland. It is one of the most ambitious biodiversity efforts ever attempted. The project will help scientists understand how species evolve and adapt to changing environments. Researchers say the genomic data will reveal how life on Earth is interconnected across the tree of life. By sequencing so many species, the project dwarfs all other efforts to conserve and catalog biodiversity. Supporters believe the knowledge gained will help save species from extinction and boost conservation. We are now entering the Sixth Mass Extinction, driven by human activity. Only by understanding every species can we hope to protect the natural world for future generations.</p>\n<h4 class=\"font-title-md text-on-surface mt-4 mb-2\">Recording Three</h4>\n<p>Professor Tom Oliver challenges the idea that we exist as independent selves. He argues that the belief in a continuous, unchanging self is a delusion to be disposed of. He has found that our inner self and material surroundings are constantly renewed. The human body contains around 37 trillion cells, but most have a lifespan of just a few days or weeks. There is no part of your body that has existed for more than ten years. Professor Oliver concludes that seeing how interconnected we are should change how we treat each other and the planet. Only by recognizing these deep connections can humanity hope to build a sustainable future together.</p>",
